data.content:
Yes — MSTest v3.3+ introduced **typed `DynamicData`** via `Func` delegates, and MSTest 3.8+ added **`[TestDataSource<T>]`**. The cleanest modern approach:

**Option 1: Typed `DynamicData` (MSTest ≥ 3.3)**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
[DynamicData<string, bool>(nameof(TestData))]
public void Validate_ReturnsExpected(string input, bool expected)
{
    var result = Validator.IsValid(input);
    Assert.AreEqual(expected, result);
}

public static IEnumerable<(string, bool)> TestData =>
[
    ("valid@email.com", true),
    ("invalid", false),
    ("", false),
];
```

**Option 2: Custom `TestDataSource<T>` (MSTest ≥ 3.8)**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
[TestDataSource<ValidatorTestData>]
public void Validate_ReturnsExpected(string input, bool expected)
{
    var result = Validator.IsValid(input);
    Assert.AreEqual(expected, result);
}

public class ValidatorTestData : ITestDataSource<(string Input, bool Expected)>
{
    public IEnumerable<(string Input, bool Expected)> GetData()
    {
        yield return ("valid@email.com", true);
        yield return ("invalid", false);
        yield return ("", false);
    }

    public string GetDisplayName((string Input, bool Expected) data)
        => $"Input: {data.Input}";
}
```

**Key benefits:**
- **Compile-time safety** — no more `object[]` casting mismatches
- **Tuples** replace anonymous arrays, catching type/order errors at build time
- **Option 2** also gives you reusable data sources and custom display names

Option 1 is the lowest-friction upgrade from what you have today.
